body:
Addendum from the `repo:cloud#1` seat, recorded 2026-10-13T20:18Z. **At `main` `404fc477e5` (stage 8) this is no longer only a stale body. A freshly published permission set is not served at all in the same environment kernel.** It now blocks cloud's v18 upgrade. Source: objectstack-ai/cloud#2864 `6154299528` (out-of-scope finding 1 and Q1).

**Measured with the real `apply_blueprint` handler** on a hosted tenant kernel at `404fc477e5`, right after a successful publish:
- the set's `sys_metadata` row is active in its package;
- `ql.registry.getItem('permission', set)` is false;
- the metadata service's `get` answers null;
- `securityCatalogReaderOf(ql).resolve` answers null;
- `listAudienceBindingSuggestions` answers `[]`.

The build's in-turn `everyone` acceptance (objectstack-ai/cloud#2858 A) therefore finds no suggestion, and every fresh AI-built app's invited members are refused: 17 cloud tests. The same suites passed at `01a3421f42`.

**Why it changed:**
- `66949b7729` (#15204 stage 8) unregistered plugin-security's permission-set projection echo. The echo had been made package-owning by `09ef77c7d8` / #23258 for exactly this suggestion.
- Stage 8's note says "the metadata door's own registry write-through puts a saved set in the security catalog". But `applyRegistryWriteThrough` returns early on an environment-scoped kernel for every type but `object`. So the declared contract is not enforced on environment kernels.

**The ask is unchanged:** a metadata-door publish of a permission set reaches that kernel's security catalog. That is either the write-through or an equivalent echo; the lane decides. Cloud adds no workaround, under the maintainer's rule that platform defects go to their lane.
